11:11:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The sonic boom isn't about speed alone—it's about a *physical object* pushing through *air* faster than sound can get out of its way. Here's what actually happens with a jet: as it flies, it pushes air molecules aside, and those air molecules bump into their neighbors, creating pressure waves that spread out at the speed of sound. Normally these waves race ahead of the jet, smoothly parting the air before it arrives. But if the jet itself is moving faster than those pressure waves can travel, the waves pile up on top of each other into a shockwave—a sudden wall of compressed air. When that shockwave passes over you, you hear it as a sharp "boom." The key ingredients are: (1) something physically moving through a medium like air, and (2) that thing displacing/compressing the medium as it goes, faster than the medium's own signal speed (sound). Light and radio waves break this completely: - They're not physical objects pushing through air. A photon doesn't shove air molecules out of its path the way a wing or a bullet does. - They don't need air to travel at all—they move through empty vacuum just fine, and they'd propagate at the same speed whether air is there or not. - There's no "compression" or "displacement" of the medium happening, so there's nothing to pile up into a shockwave. So it's not that light is "too fast" and somehow skips past the phenomenon—it's that a sonic boom requires a specific mechanical interaction between a moving object and a medium it's disturbing. Light doesn't interact with air that way, so the whole setup for a boom just doesn't apply. If you want a rough analogy: it's like asking why a ripple you make by clapping your hands doesn't cause a traffic jam on the highway. Traffic jams require cars interacting with each other on a road; your clap has nothing to do with that system at all.
